  "narrative": "These proposals extend the currently accepted dataset associations rather than repeating them. The KEAP1/NFE2L2 reversed association is now registered in the correct direction in H13; the new interaction and library-restricted claims ask whether it is stronger in adenocarcinoma, whether it remains large in the dominant Avana screen library, and whether it is robust across measured screening context. Similarly, SMARCA4-loss-driven SMARCA2 dependency is tested for lineage heterogeneity and Avana-library robustness. MSI-high association with WRN is also checked within Avana, while additional DNA-repair hypotheses (BRCA2 loss with WRN/DHX9 and MSI-high with USP7) broaden the DNA damage response exploration. Reciprocal receptor-tyrosine-kinase hypotheses test amplification-specific cross dependencies: EGFR amplification associated with weaker ERBB2 dependency and ERBB2 amplification associated with weaker EGFR dependency. Finally, two unassigned marker claims probe whether research marker D or F predicts SMARCA2 or WRN dependency; their expected raw direction is deliberately uncertain, with the registered direction testing stronger dependency.",
  "research_notes": "Current accepted associations remain H9 SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency; H10 MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency; H13 KEAP1 loss -> higher/weaker NFE2L2 dependency. H4 EGFR main EGFR dependency is unresolved/closed after weak validation. New wave adds subtype and library robustness for accepted effects, reciprocal EGFR/ERBB2 dependency specificity, BRCA2/DNA repair dependencies, USP7-MSI, and unassigned marker exploration. Next analysis should prioritize accepted-effect refinements plus any marker/cross-RTK claims that may reveal large effects."
